6. HEADLINE ANSWER AND WHAT IS / IS NOT SUPPORTED
   HEADLINE: summed social capital peaks in the century bin 1400-1301 BCE = 14th century BCE (sum 4.238 Z; next best 13th century BCE 3.933).
   RAW-GRID TIE: the uninterpolated 100-yr source grid gives an identical maximum (4.238) in ['14th century BCE', '13th century BCE'], i.e. ['1400-1301 BCE', '1300-1201 BCE'].
   Because the source data ARE 100-yr bins, the defensible statement is the plateau 1400-1200 BCE (peak centred on the 14th c. BCE); any single-century label is convention-dependent.
   Components with a ROBUST full-span increase: ['Copper&Gold', 'MonumentCount']; components increasing BASELINE->PEAK: ['Amber', 'Copper&Gold', 'MonumentCount'].
   Increasing by BOTH definitions: Copper&Gold, MonumentCount
   Increasing ONLY relative to the pre-peak baseline (NOT a robust full-span increase): Amber (own max 33rd century BCE +1.54; only 7% of the summed rise; rank 14/37 in its own series in the peak century; full-span sign not robust)
   Increasing by NEITHER definition: none
   Share of the summed rise into the peak (baseline->peak): Copper&Gold 62%, Amber 7%, MonumentCount 35%.
   Pollen openness: covariate only; shared window is truncated at 702 BCE and its own maximum sits on
   that edge, so it is used ONLY to note that openness is mid-range at the social-capital peak, NOT to
   date a demographic maximum. No dating uncertainty, replication or significance testing exists in
   these files, so every statement above is descriptive (ranking / plateau), never inferential.
